{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`src/rebar/_deprecations.py:94-112` schedules exactly three public removals: the Python-library `rebar.transition(force_close=...)` keyword, boolean `rebar.transition(force=True)`, and explicit hosted-OpenAI `openai-chat:` model selection. The Responses migration `upbeat-illadvised-springtail` is closed, so this deliberately pre-1.0 contract break is now due. The CLI has already rejected the retired close spellings in `src/rebar/_commands/transition.py`; the MCP transition schema never exposed `force_close`, so neither surface is a removal target.\n\n## End State\n\nThe Python library accepts only a non-empty, reason-bearing string `force=\"<reason>\"`; passing boolean `force=True` or the `force_close` keyword fails at that library boundary and names `force=\"<reason>\"` as the replacement. Hosted models use `openai-responses:<model>`; the four hosted `gpt-5.4*` entries in `.github/llm-providers/openai.toml` are migrated. A custom OpenAI-compatible `base_url` continues using the Chat Completions dialect and may retain the internal explicit Chat routing it needs.\n\n## Implementation Plan\n\n1. Remove the two library compatibility branches and their scheduled deprecation entries. Do not add an environment/configuration tombstone: these are Python call-signature inputs, so rejection belongs at the callable boundary and does not create persisted configuration debt.\n2. Remove hosted-only explicit `openai-chat:` acceptance/warning paths and migrate provider inventories, examples, and default model configuration to `openai-responses:`. Preserve custom-endpoint dialect selection and its tests.\n3. Update maintained API, provider, schema/reference, changelog, and release documentation. Historical event fields such as `force_close_reason` remain unchanged because they are durable state, not a public input alias.\n4. Add focused library/provider regression coverage for rejection messages, canonical replacements, configuration migration, and custom endpoint behavior.\n\n## File Impact\n\n- `src/rebar/_deprecations.py`, `src/rebar/_lib_writes.py` — scheduled inputs and library-boundary validation\n- `src/rebar/llm/runner.py`, `src/rebar/llm/model_classes.py`, `src/rebar/llm/config.py`, `.github/llm-providers/openai.toml` — hosted provider selection and deployed model inventory\n- maintained provider/API docs, generated references, `CHANGELOG.md`, and `docs/release-notes.md` — canonical migration guidance\n- focused transition and LLM configuration/provider tests\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `rebar.transition(force_close=...)` and `rebar.transition(force=True)` are rejected at the Python-library boundary with an error naming `force=\"<explicit reason>\"`; `force=\"<explicit reason>\"` still succeeds.\n- [ ] The CLI remains on its existing rejection path and the MCP schema remains free of `force_close`; this change does not introduce a second compatibility removal or a new bypass.\n- [ ] Hosted explicit `openai-chat:<model>` is rejected with `openai-responses:<model>` named as its replacement, while `openai-responses:<model>` succeeds.\n- [ ] All hosted `gpt-5.4*` entries in `.github/llm-providers/openai.toml` use `openai-responses:`.\n- [ ] A configured custom OpenAI-compatible endpoint retains Chat Completions routing; its behavior is not changed by the hosted-prefix removal.\n- [ ] Maintained documentation and generated references name the canonical replacements; historical durable `force_close_reason` state remains intact.\n- [ ] Focused library, provider/configuration, documentation-generation, lint, typecheck, and relevant schema/reference tests pass."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788368619971748001,"uuid":"b3fc4141-ad9e-4be6-b248-3223bd1ad538"}